  "theme": "directions",
  "culturalNote": "Outside the big cities, directions are given by landmarks — the shop, the wooden bridge, the primary school, the small hill — and almost never by street names. Kenyans describe a route in the present tense, step by step: \"unaenda moja kwa moja, halafu unageuka kushoto\" (you go straight, then you turn left). Locals are generous with directions; a greeting first (\"Habari yako, kaka?\") makes all the difference.",
  "startingPoint": "You're visiting a small town near Nyahururu and need the district hospital. A young man is standing outside a shop.",
